    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old male named James is evaluated for progressive, bilateral hearing loss that has developed over the past several years. He reports having particular difficulty following conversations in public settings. Audiology confirms a diagnosis of presbycusis. What is the primary underlying pathophysiology of this age-related sensorineural hearing change?', '["Stiffening of the tympanic membrane and calcification of the ossicular chain (particularly the stapes footplate)", "Excessive accumulation of endolymph within the semicircular canals and cochlear duct", "Degeneration and progressive loss of sensory hair cells (primarily outer hair cells) and spiral ganglion neurons, starting in the basal turn of the organ of Corti", "Atrophy of the primary auditory cortex and loss of temporal lobe neurons with normal cochlear function", "Chronic low-grade inflammation of the eustachian tube leading to bilateral middle ear effusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Presbycusis is the most common form of age-related sensorineural hearing loss in older adults. The primary pathophysiology involves the **progressive degeneration and loss of sensory hair cells** (especially outer hair cells, then inner hair cells) and **spiral ganglion neurons**, beginning in the **basal turn of the organ of Corti** (which detects high-frequency sounds). Stiffening of the basilar membrane and atrophy of the stria vascularis (strial or metabolic presbycusis, which causes a flat hearing loss pattern) also contribute. Tympanic membrane stiffening is not the primary mechanism of presbycusis, nor is middle ear effusion. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
