    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named Donald is evaluated for progressive, bilateral hearing loss that has developed over the past several years. He reports having particular difficulty following conversations in public settings. Audiology confirms a diagnosis of presbycusis. What is the primary underlying pathophysiology of this age-related sensorineural hearing change?', '["Atrophy of the primary auditory cortex and loss of temporal lobe neurons with normal cochlear function", "Degeneration and progressive loss of sensory hair cells (primarily outer hair cells) and spiral ganglion neurons, starting in the basal turn of the organ of Corti", "Chronic low-grade inflammation of the eustachian tube leading to bilateral middle ear effusion", "Stiffening of the tympanic membrane and calcification of the ossicular chain (particularly the stapes footplate)", "Excessive accumulation of endolymph within the semicircular canals and cochlear duct"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Presbycusis is the most common form of age-related sensorineural hearing loss in older adults. The primary pathophysiology involves the **progressive degeneration and loss of sensory hair cells** (especially outer hair cells, then inner hair cells) and **spiral ganglion neurons**, beginning in the **basal turn of the organ of Corti** (which detects high-frequency sounds). Stiffening of the basilar membrane and atrophy of the stria vascularis (strial or metabolic presbycusis, which causes a flat hearing loss pattern) also contribute. Tympanic membrane stiffening is not the primary mechanism of presbycusis, nor is middle ear effusion. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Henry is evaluated for progressive, bilateral hearing loss that has developed over the past several years. He reports having particular difficulty following conversations in public settings. Audiology confirms a diagnosis of presbycusis. What is the primary underlying pathophysiology of this age-related sensorineural hearing change?', '["Stiffening of the tympanic membrane and calcification of the ossicular chain (particularly the stapes footplate)", "Atrophy of the primary auditory cortex and loss of temporal lobe neurons with normal cochlear function", "Excessive accumulation of endolymph within the semicircular canals and cochlear duct", "Chronic low-grade inflammation of the eustachian tube leading to bilateral middle ear effusion", "Degeneration and progressive loss of sensory hair cells (primarily outer hair cells) and spiral ganglion neurons, starting in the basal turn of the organ of Corti"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Presbycusis is the most common form of age-related sensorineural hearing loss in older adults. The primary pathophysiology involves the **progressive degeneration and loss of sensory hair cells** (especially outer hair cells, then inner hair cells) and **spiral ganglion neurons**, beginning in the **basal turn of the organ of Corti** (which detects high-frequency sounds). Stiffening of the basilar membrane and atrophy of the stria vascularis (strial or metabolic presbycusis, which causes a flat hearing loss pattern) also contribute. Tympanic membrane stiffening is not the primary mechanism of presbycusis, nor is middle ear effusion. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old male named David presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named James presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old male named Albert presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies", "Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male named Robert presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment", "Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old male named Clarence presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old male named Henry presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old female named Eleanor presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. She reports that she can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Grace presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. She reports that she can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old male named Herbert presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies", "Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named Thomas presents to the clinic with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing difficulty. He reports that he can hear that people are speaking but struggles to comprehend the specific words. An audiometry test is performed. Which of the following audiometric patterns is most characteristic of age-related presbycusis?', '["Bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with relatively preserved low-frequency thresholds", "Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss demonstrating a sharp, localized notch at 4000 Hz with normal high frequencies", "Fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss affecting both high and low frequencies, associated with persistent episodic vertigo", "Unilateral, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with prominent acoustic recruitment", "Bilateral, symmetric conductive hearing loss with a maximum air-bone gap of 40 dB across all frequencies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Age-related presbycusis classically presents as a **bilateral, symmetric, sloping high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**. Low frequencies are typically preserved in the early to moderate stages of the disease. Unilateral or highly asymmetric loss should prompt investigation for other etiologies, such as vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma). A localized 4000 Hz notch is characteristic of noise-induced hearing loss (trauma), whereas fluctuating sensorineural loss with vertigo is characteristic of Meniere''s disease. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old female named Louise reports difficulty understanding speech, particularly when attending family gatherings or sitting in noisy restaurants. Louise notes that people seem to ''mumble'' and that she can hear their voices but cannot make out the words. Which of the following best explains this clinical phenomenon in presbycusis?', '["Transient vestibular dysfunction that causes visual and auditory stimuli to become uncoupled in noisy settings", "Degeneration of the low-frequency hair cells in the apex of the cochlea, which are responsible for detecting the overall loudness of speech", "A central processing deficit in the primary auditory cortex that specifically prevents the parsing of low-frequency vowel sounds", "Excessive acoustic impedance in the middle ear due to fluid accumulation that dampens all incoming speech sounds equally", "Selective loss of high-frequency hearing impairs the perception of voiceless consonants (e.g., s, f, t, p, k, sh, th), which carry the primary information for speech clarity and word discrimination"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Speech is composed of low-frequency vowels (which provide volume) and high-frequency consonants (which provide clarity and word discrimination). Voiceless consonants like *s, f, t, p, k, sh, th* operate at higher frequencies (2000-8000 Hz). Because presbycusis selectively degrades high-frequency hearing, older adults lose the ability to distinguish these consonants. Consequently, speech sounds loud enough (due to preserved low-frequency vowels) but lacks clarity (''people are mumbling''), especially in environments with background noise (cocktail party effect). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Evelyn reports difficulty understanding speech, particularly when attending family gatherings or sitting in noisy restaurants. Evelyn notes that people seem to ''mumble'' and that she can hear their voices but cannot make out the words. Which of the following best explains this clinical phenomenon in presbycusis?', '["Transient vestibular dysfunction that causes visual and auditory stimuli to become uncoupled in noisy settings", "Degeneration of the low-frequency hair cells in the apex of the cochlea, which are responsible for detecting the overall loudness of speech", "A central processing deficit in the primary auditory cortex that specifically prevents the parsing of low-frequency vowel sounds", "Selective loss of high-frequency hearing impairs the perception of voiceless consonants (e.g., s, f, t, p, k, sh, th), which carry the primary information for speech clarity and word discrimination", "Excessive acoustic impedance in the middle ear due to fluid accumulation that dampens all incoming speech sounds equally"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Speech is composed of low-frequency vowels (which provide volume) and high-frequency consonants (which provide clarity and word discrimination). Voiceless consonants like *s, f, t, p, k, sh, th* operate at higher frequencies (2000-8000 Hz). Because presbycusis selectively degrades high-frequency hearing, older adults lose the ability to distinguish these consonants. Consequently, speech sounds loud enough (due to preserved low-frequency vowels) but lacks clarity (''people are mumbling''), especially in environments with background noise (cocktail party effect). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
