    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Harry with moderate presbycusis is fitted with a digital hearing aid. After 4 weeks of use, he complains that although he can hear better in quiet rooms, he still struggles significantly in noisy social settings because the device amplifies background noise. Which of the following hearing aid technologies or adjustments is most appropriate to address his complaint?', '["Increasing the overall gain of the hearing aid across all frequencies to overpower background noise", "Disabling the digital noise reduction algorithms to allow natural sound filtering by the brainstem", "Switching from a digital hearing aid to an analog hearing aid with a bone-conduction receiver", "Activation of a directional microphone system that prioritizes sounds coming from the front while attenuating noise from the sides and behind", "Recommending a monaural hearing aid fitting instead of a bilateral fitting to reduce total auditory input"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Modern digital hearing aids use **directional microphones** to help older adults with presbycusis distinguish speech from background noise. By prioritizing acoustic inputs from the front (where the speaker is typically located) and attenuating ambient noise from the sides and rear, directional microphones improve the signal-to-noise ratio. Other helpful features include digital noise reduction and feedback cancellation. Monaural fitting is inferior to binaural fitting for speech localization and understanding in noise. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old male named Kenneth with moderate presbycusis is fitted with a digital hearing aid. After 3 weeks of use, he complains that although he can hear better in quiet rooms, he still struggles significantly in noisy social settings because the device amplifies background noise. Which of the following hearing aid technologies or adjustments is most appropriate to address his complaint?', '["Increasing the overall gain of the hearing aid across all frequencies to overpower background noise", "Recommending a monaural hearing aid fitting instead of a bilateral fitting to reduce total auditory input", "Switching from a digital hearing aid to an analog hearing aid with a bone-conduction receiver", "Activation of a directional microphone system that prioritizes sounds coming from the front while attenuating noise from the sides and behind", "Disabling the digital noise reduction algorithms to allow natural sound filtering by the brainstem"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Modern digital hearing aids use **directional microphones** to help older adults with presbycusis distinguish speech from background noise. By prioritizing acoustic inputs from the front (where the speaker is typically located) and attenuating ambient noise from the sides and rear, directional microphones improve the signal-to-noise ratio. Other helpful features include digital noise reduction and feedback cancellation. Monaural fitting is inferior to binaural fitting for speech localization and understanding in noise. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
