    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old female named Eleanor with moderate presbycusis is fitted with a digital hearing aid. After 6 weeks of use, she complains that although she can hear better in quiet rooms, she still struggles significantly in noisy social settings because the device amplifies background noise. Which of the following hearing aid technologies or adjustments is most appropriate to address her complaint?', '["Activation of a directional microphone system that prioritizes sounds coming from the front while attenuating noise from the sides and behind", "Switching from a digital hearing aid to an analog hearing aid with a bone-conduction receiver", "Increasing the overall gain of the hearing aid across all frequencies to overpower background noise", "Disabling the digital noise reduction algorithms to allow natural sound filtering by the brainstem", "Recommending a monaural hearing aid fitting instead of a bilateral fitting to reduce total auditory input"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Modern digital hearing aids use **directional microphones** to help older adults with presbycusis distinguish speech from background noise. By prioritizing acoustic inputs from the front (where the speaker is typically located) and attenuating ambient noise from the sides and rear, directional microphones improve the signal-to-noise ratio. Other helpful features include digital noise reduction and feedback cancellation. Monaural fitting is inferior to binaural fitting for speech localization and understanding in noise. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old male named Ralph with moderate presbycusis is fitted with a digital hearing aid. After 2 weeks of use, he complains that although he can hear better in quiet rooms, he still struggles significantly in noisy social settings because the device amplifies background noise. Which of the following hearing aid technologies or adjustments is most appropriate to address his complaint?', '["Activation of a directional microphone system that prioritizes sounds coming from the front while attenuating noise from the sides and behind", "Disabling the digital noise reduction algorithms to allow natural sound filtering by the brainstem", "Increasing the overall gain of the hearing aid across all frequencies to overpower background noise", "Recommending a monaural hearing aid fitting instead of a bilateral fitting to reduce total auditory input", "Switching from a digital hearing aid to an analog hearing aid with a bone-conduction receiver"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Modern digital hearing aids use **directional microphones** to help older adults with presbycusis distinguish speech from background noise. By prioritizing acoustic inputs from the front (where the speaker is typically located) and attenuating ambient noise from the sides and rear, directional microphones improve the signal-to-noise ratio. Other helpful features include digital noise reduction and feedback cancellation. Monaural fitting is inferior to binaural fitting for speech localization and understanding in noise. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old male named Arthur residing in an assisted living facility has severe presbycusis and struggles to participate in group lectures or hear the television, even when wearing properly fitted digital hearing aids. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to improve his functional communication and quality of life?', '["Refer the patient for immediate surgical middle ear ossicular reconstruction to bypass the sensorineural loss", "Increase the hearing aid volume until it produces a continuous high-pitched squeal (acoustic feedback) to ensure maximum amplification", "Introduce an Assistive Listening Device (ALD) such as a personal frequency-modulated (FM) system, infrared system, or a personal amplifier (e.g., PocketTalker)", "Discontinue the hearing aids permanently and initiate speech-reading (lip-reading) training as the sole communication modality", "Prescribe a course of high-dose oral glucocorticoids to regenerate the degenerated auditory pathway"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When hearing aids are insufficient for functional communication, particularly in large rooms, classrooms, or for watching television, **Assistive Listening Devices (ALDs)** are indicated. These include personal frequency-modulated (FM) systems, infrared systems, or simple personal amplifiers (e.g., PocketTalker). ALDs work by bringing the sound source directly to the listener''s ear, minimizing the negative effects of distance, reverberation, and background noise. Acoustic feedback (squealing) indicates a poor mold fit or excessive volume and is undesirable. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
