    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Evelyn has severe-to-profound bilateral sensorineural hearing loss and derives virtually no benefit from high-power, properly fitted digital hearing aids. Her speech recognition score is 52% on standardized sentence tests. She is otherwise cognitively intact and medically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?', '["Advise the patient that cochlear implantation is strictly contraindicated in individuals aged >= 65 due to poor neuroplasticity", "Refer to an otolaryngologist for cochlear implant evaluation; older adults can achieve significant improvements in speech perception and quality of life, regardless of advanced age", "Prescribe a trial of hyperbaric oxygen therapy to regenerate the damaged hair cells of the organ of Corti", "Suggest bilateral middle ear implants (vibrant soundbridge) as they are superior to cochlear implants for profound sensorineural loss", "Recommend continuing with non-functioning hearing aids and starting low-dose antidepressant therapy to manage sensory deprivation"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Cochlear implants** are the standard of care for adults with severe-to-profound bilateral sensorineural hearing loss who receive limited benefit from hearing aids (defined typically as speech/sentence recognition scores <= 50-60%). Advanced age is **not a contraindication** to cochlear implantation; studies show that older adults (even octogenarians and nonagenarians) experience significant gains in speech understanding, functional independence, and quality of life, with surgical complication rates comparable to younger cohorts. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; JAMA Otolaryngol 2018)'),
