    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Jean has severe-to-profound bilateral sensorineural hearing loss and derives virtually no benefit from high-power, properly fitted digital hearing aids. Her speech recognition score is 52% on standardized sentence tests. She is otherwise cognitively intact and medically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?', '["Advise the patient that cochlear implantation is strictly contraindicated in individuals aged >= 65 due to poor neuroplasticity", "Suggest bilateral middle ear implants (vibrant soundbridge) as they are superior to cochlear implants for profound sensorineural loss", "Prescribe a trial of hyperbaric oxygen therapy to regenerate the damaged hair cells of the organ of Corti", "Refer to an otolaryngologist for cochlear implant evaluation; older adults can achieve significant improvements in speech perception and quality of life, regardless of advanced age", "Recommend continuing with non-functioning hearing aids and starting low-dose antidepressant therapy to manage sensory deprivation"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Cochlear implants** are the standard of care for adults with severe-to-profound bilateral sensorineural hearing loss who receive limited benefit from hearing aids (defined typically as speech/sentence recognition scores <= 50-60%). Advanced age is **not a contraindication** to cochlear implantation; studies show that older adults (even octogenarians and nonagenarians) experience significant gains in speech understanding, functional independence, and quality of life, with surgical complication rates comparable to younger cohorts. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; JAMA Otolaryngol 2018)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old female named Florence is hospitalized for a severe pseudomonal urinary tract infection and treated with intravenous amikacin. On day 6 of therapy, she complains of new-onset bilateral high-pitched tinnitus and a feeling of imbalance. What is the mechanism of action of the drug causing this adverse effect, and what is the target tissue?', '["Cisplatin-induced cross-linking of DNA in the spiral ganglion neurons of the cochlear apex", "Aminoglycoside-induced cochleotoxicity and vestibulotoxicity via accumulation in the endolymph, leading to reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation and apoptosis of sensory hair cells", "Antihistamine-induced anticholinergic blockade of the vestibular nuclei in the brainstem causing sensory conflict", "Salicylate-induced inhibition of cyclooxygenase, disrupting the cochlear microcirculation and causing reversible tinnitus", "Loop diuretic-induced edema of the stria vascularis, altering the endolymphatic potential and blocking acoustic transduction"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Aminoglycosides** (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) are well-known ototoxic agents. They are cleared slowly from the inner ear fluids (endolymph and perilymph), leading to accumulation. Once inside sensory hair cells, they generate **reactive oxygen species (ROS)**, which trigger cascade pathways resulting in **outer hair cell apoptosis**. Gentamicin and tobramycin are primarily vestibulotoxic (causing ataxia, oscillopsia, and gait instability), whereas amikacin and neomycin are primarily cochleotoxic (causing permanent high-frequency hearing loss and tinnitus). Risk factors include prolonged therapy, renal impairment, and concomitant use of other ototoxic drugs (e.g., loop diuretics). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male named Charles is hospitalized for a severe pseudomonal urinary tract infection and treated with intravenous tobramycin. On day 7 of therapy, he complains of new-onset bilateral high-pitched tinnitus and a feeling of imbalance. What is the mechanism of action of the drug causing this adverse effect, and what is the target tissue?', '["Aminoglycoside-induced cochleotoxicity and vestibulotoxicity via accumulation in the endolymph, leading to reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation and apoptosis of sensory hair cells", "Antihistamine-induced anticholinergic blockade of the vestibular nuclei in the brainstem causing sensory conflict", "Salicylate-induced inhibition of cyclooxygenase, disrupting the cochlear microcirculation and causing reversible tinnitus", "Cisplatin-induced cross-linking of DNA in the spiral ganglion neurons of the cochlear apex", "Loop diuretic-induced edema of the stria vascularis, altering the endolymphatic potential and blocking acoustic transduction"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Aminoglycosides** (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) are well-known ototoxic agents. They are cleared slowly from the inner ear fluids (endolymph and perilymph), leading to accumulation. Once inside sensory hair cells, they generate **reactive oxygen species (ROS)**, which trigger cascade pathways resulting in **outer hair cell apoptosis**. Gentamicin and tobramycin are primarily vestibulotoxic (causing ataxia, oscillopsia, and gait instability), whereas amikacin and neomycin are primarily cochleotoxic (causing permanent high-frequency hearing loss and tinnitus). Risk factors include prolonged therapy, renal impairment, and concomitant use of other ototoxic drugs (e.g., loop diuretics). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
