    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old female named Frances is hospitalized for a severe pseudomonal urinary tract infection and treated with intravenous tobramycin. On day 7 of therapy, she complains of new-onset bilateral high-pitched tinnitus and a feeling of imbalance. What is the mechanism of action of the drug causing this adverse effect, and what is the target tissue?', '["Salicylate-induced inhibition of cyclooxygenase, disrupting the cochlear microcirculation and causing reversible tinnitus", "Antihistamine-induced anticholinergic blockade of the vestibular nuclei in the brainstem causing sensory conflict", "Cisplatin-induced cross-linking of DNA in the spiral ganglion neurons of the cochlear apex", "Aminoglycoside-induced cochleotoxicity and vestibulotoxicity via accumulation in the endolymph, leading to reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation and apoptosis of sensory hair cells", "Loop diuretic-induced edema of the stria vascularis, altering the endolymphatic potential and blocking acoustic transduction"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Aminoglycosides** (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) are well-known ototoxic agents. They are cleared slowly from the inner ear fluids (endolymph and perilymph), leading to accumulation. Once inside sensory hair cells, they generate **reactive oxygen species (ROS)**, which trigger cascade pathways resulting in **outer hair cell apoptosis**. Gentamicin and tobramycin are primarily vestibulotoxic (causing ataxia, oscillopsia, and gait instability), whereas amikacin and neomycin are primarily cochleotoxic (causing permanent high-frequency hearing loss and tinnitus). Risk factors include prolonged therapy, renal impairment, and concomitant use of other ototoxic drugs (e.g., loop diuretics). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Lillian with chronic kidney disease stage G4 is admitted for an acute flare of congestive heart failure. She is started on high-dose intravenous bumetanide. During the rapid infusion, she complains of sudden hearing loss and roaring tinnitus. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology and clinical course of this ototoxic event?', '["Loop diuretics inhibit the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the stria vascularis of the cochlea, disrupting the endolymphatic potential; this effect is typically transient and reversible but can be permanent with rapid high-dose infusions, especially in renal impairment", "Loop diuretics cause central auditory processing delays by blocking calcium channels in the olivocochlear bundle", "Loop diuretics cause a conductive hearing loss by promoting fluid accumulation (effusion) within the middle ear cleft", "Loop diuretics inhibit cochlear cyclooxygenase, leading to chronic vasoconstriction of the internal auditory artery", "Loop diuretics cause irreversible degeneration of the spiral ganglion neurons in the basal turn of the cochlea"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide, ethacrynic acid) cause ototoxicity by inhibiting the **Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC1)** in the **stria vascularis** of the cochlea. This disrupts the ion transport required to maintain the high potassium concentration in the endolymph, thereby abolishing the endocochlear potential and causing hearing loss and tinnitus. This ototoxicity is **usually transient and reversible** once the drug is discontinued. However, it can become **permanent**, particularly when administered as a rapid IV bolus at high doses, in patients with chronic kidney disease (where drug clearance is impaired), or when co-administered with other ototoxic drugs like aminoglycosides. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old male named Albert with chronic kidney disease stage G3b is admitted for an acute flare of congestive heart failure. He is started on high-dose intravenous ethacrynic acid. During the rapid infusion, he complains of sudden hearing loss and roaring tinnitus. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology and clinical course of this ototoxic event?', '["Loop diuretics inhibit cochlear cyclooxygenase, leading to chronic vasoconstriction of the internal auditory artery", "Loop diuretics inhibit the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the stria vascularis of the cochlea, disrupting the endolymphatic potential; this effect is typically transient and reversible but can be permanent with rapid high-dose infusions, especially in renal impairment", "Loop diuretics cause central auditory processing delays by blocking calcium channels in the olivocochlear bundle", "Loop diuretics cause irreversible degeneration of the spiral ganglion neurons in the basal turn of the cochlea", "Loop diuretics cause a conductive hearing loss by promoting fluid accumulation (effusion) within the middle ear cleft"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide, ethacrynic acid) cause ototoxicity by inhibiting the **Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC1)** in the **stria vascularis** of the cochlea. This disrupts the ion transport required to maintain the high potassium concentration in the endolymph, thereby abolishing the endocochlear potential and causing hearing loss and tinnitus. This ototoxicity is **usually transient and reversible** once the drug is discontinued. However, it can become **permanent**, particularly when administered as a rapid IV bolus at high doses, in patients with chronic kidney disease (where drug clearance is impaired), or when co-administered with other ototoxic drugs like aminoglycosides. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
