    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old female named Lillian with chronic kidney disease stage G3b is admitted for an acute flare of congestive heart failure. She is started on high-dose intravenous ethacrynic acid. During the rapid infusion, she complains of sudden hearing loss and roaring tinnitus. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology and clinical course of this ototoxic event?', '["Loop diuretics inhibit the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the stria vascularis of the cochlea, disrupting the endolymphatic potential; this effect is typically transient and reversible but can be permanent with rapid high-dose infusions, especially in renal impairment", "Loop diuretics cause a conductive hearing loss by promoting fluid accumulation (effusion) within the middle ear cleft", "Loop diuretics inhibit cochlear cyclooxygenase, leading to chronic vasoconstriction of the internal auditory artery", "Loop diuretics cause central auditory processing delays by blocking calcium channels in the olivocochlear bundle", "Loop diuretics cause irreversible degeneration of the spiral ganglion neurons in the basal turn of the cochlea"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide, ethacrynic acid) cause ototoxicity by inhibiting the **Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC1)** in the **stria vascularis** of the cochlea. This disrupts the ion transport required to maintain the high potassium concentration in the endolymph, thereby abolishing the endocochlear potential and causing hearing loss and tinnitus. This ototoxicity is **usually transient and reversible** once the drug is discontinued. However, it can become **permanent**, particularly when administered as a rapid IV bolus at high doses, in patients with chronic kidney disease (where drug clearance is impaired), or when co-administered with other ototoxic drugs like aminoglycosides. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
