    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male named George with chronic kidney disease stage G3b is admitted for an acute flare of congestive heart failure. He is started on high-dose intravenous bumetanide. During the rapid infusion, he complains of sudden hearing loss and roaring tinnitus. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology and clinical course of this ototoxic event?', '["Loop diuretics cause irreversible degeneration of the spiral ganglion neurons in the basal turn of the cochlea", "Loop diuretics cause a conductive hearing loss by promoting fluid accumulation (effusion) within the middle ear cleft", "Loop diuretics inhibit cochlear cyclooxygenase, leading to chronic vasoconstriction of the internal auditory artery", "Loop diuretics inhibit the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the stria vascularis of the cochlea, disrupting the endolymphatic potential; this effect is typically transient and reversible but can be permanent with rapid high-dose infusions, especially in renal impairment", "Loop diuretics cause central auditory processing delays by blocking calcium channels in the olivocochlear bundle"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide, ethacrynic acid) cause ototoxicity by inhibiting the **Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC1)** in the **stria vascularis** of the cochlea. This disrupts the ion transport required to maintain the high potassium concentration in the endolymph, thereby abolishing the endocochlear potential and causing hearing loss and tinnitus. This ototoxicity is **usually transient and reversible** once the drug is discontinued. However, it can become **permanent**, particularly when administered as a rapid IV bolus at high doses, in patients with chronic kidney disease (where drug clearance is impaired), or when co-administered with other ototoxic drugs like aminoglycosides. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Marie with chronic kidney disease stage G3b is admitted for an acute flare of congestive heart failure. She is started on high-dose intravenous furosemide. During the rapid infusion, she complains of sudden hearing loss and roaring tinnitus. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology and clinical course of this ototoxic event?', '["Loop diuretics inhibit the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the stria vascularis of the cochlea, disrupting the endolymphatic potential; this effect is typically transient and reversible but can be permanent with rapid high-dose infusions, especially in renal impairment", "Loop diuretics cause irreversible degeneration of the spiral ganglion neurons in the basal turn of the cochlea", "Loop diuretics cause central auditory processing delays by blocking calcium channels in the olivocochlear bundle", "Loop diuretics cause a conductive hearing loss by promoting fluid accumulation (effusion) within the middle ear cleft", "Loop diuretics inhibit cochlear cyclooxygenase, leading to chronic vasoconstriction of the internal auditory artery"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide, ethacrynic acid) cause ototoxicity by inhibiting the **Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC1)** in the **stria vascularis** of the cochlea. This disrupts the ion transport required to maintain the high potassium concentration in the endolymph, thereby abolishing the endocochlear potential and causing hearing loss and tinnitus. This ototoxicity is **usually transient and reversible** once the drug is discontinued. However, it can become **permanent**, particularly when administered as a rapid IV bolus at high doses, in patients with chronic kidney disease (where drug clearance is impaired), or when co-administered with other ototoxic drugs like aminoglycosides. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female named Ruby with severe osteoarthritis has been self-medicating with high doses of over-the-counter salicylates (taking 3.5 g daily). She presents with bilateral, continuous high-pitched tinnitus and mild, flat sensorineural hearing loss. What is the most appropriate clinical reasoning and management?', '["Salicylates cause central vestibular neuropathy; obtain a brain MRI to evaluate for cerebellar demyelination and stop the drug", "The symptoms are caused by salicylate-induced chronic otitis media; prescribe a 10-day course of oral amoxicillin-clavulanate", "Salicylate ototoxicity is caused by reversible inhibition of prestin (the motor protein of outer hair cells) and altered arachidonic acid metabolism; the symptoms are completely reversible and will resolve within 24-72 hours of discontinuing the drug", "Instruct the patient to double the salicylate dose to suppress the auditory phantom perception (tinnitus) via NMDA blockade", "Salicylates cause permanent necrosis of the inner hair cells in the apex of the cochlea; the hearing loss is irreversible, and the patient should be referred for immediate hearing aid fitting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'High-dose **salicylates (aspirin)** cause ototoxicity characterized by **bilateral high-pitched tinnitus** and mild-to-moderate symmetric sensorineural hearing loss. The pathophysiology involves: (1) Inhibition of **prestin**, a motor protein in outer hair cells responsible for electromotility (cochlear amplifier), which dampens cochlear sensitivity, and (2) Alterations in arachidonic acid metabolism, causing NMDA receptor hyperactivation in spiral ganglion neurons. Salicylate-induced ototoxicity is **completely reversible** and typically resolves within 24 to 72 hours after discontinuing or reducing the dose. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-year-old male named James with severe osteoarthritis has been self-medicating with high doses of over-the-counter salicylates (taking 4.0 g daily). He presents with bilateral, continuous high-pitched tinnitus and mild, flat sensorineural hearing loss. What is the most appropriate clinical reasoning and management?', '["Salicylate ototoxicity is caused by reversible inhibition of prestin (the motor protein of outer hair cells) and altered arachidonic acid metabolism; the symptoms are completely reversible and will resolve within 24-72 hours of discontinuing the drug", "Salicylates cause permanent necrosis of the inner hair cells in the apex of the cochlea; the hearing loss is irreversible, and the patient should be referred for immediate hearing aid fitting", "Instruct the patient to double the salicylate dose to suppress the auditory phantom perception (tinnitus) via NMDA blockade", "The symptoms are caused by salicylate-induced chronic otitis media; prescribe a 10-day course of oral amoxicillin-clavulanate", "Salicylates cause central vestibular neuropathy; obtain a brain MRI to evaluate for cerebellar demyelination and stop the drug"]'::jsonb, 0, 'High-dose **salicylates (aspirin)** cause ototoxicity characterized by **bilateral high-pitched tinnitus** and mild-to-moderate symmetric sensorineural hearing loss. The pathophysiology involves: (1) Inhibition of **prestin**, a motor protein in outer hair cells responsible for electromotility (cochlear amplifier), which dampens cochlear sensitivity, and (2) Alterations in arachidonic acid metabolism, causing NMDA receptor hyperactivation in spiral ganglion neurons. Salicylate-induced ototoxicity is **completely reversible** and typically resolves within 24 to 72 hours after discontinuing or reducing the dose. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
