-- US MD/MBBS (4th Year) clinical case blocks - Geriatrics: Sensory changes — presbycusis, presbyopia, functional consequences - Part 1 (1-100)
BEGIN;

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_topic_id UUID;
BEGIN
    SELECT id INTO v_topic_id FROM topics WHERE slug = 'geri-sensory-changes' LIMIT 1;
    IF v_topic_id IS NULL THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Topic geri-sensory-changes not found.';
    END IF;

    DELETE FROM mcqs WHERE topic_id = v_topic_id;

• Weber test: **Lateralizes to the left ear**
• Rinne test: **Bone conduction > Air conduction in the left ear; Air conduction > Bone conduction in the opposite ear**
Otoscopy reveals a large, dark brown, solid mass occluding 100% of the external auditory canal. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the underlying physical mechanism?', '["Presbycusis causing sensorineural hearing loss; degeneration of the hair cells in the basal turn of the cochlea preventing hair cell depolarization", "Meniere''s disease causing conductive hearing loss; endolymphatic hydrops disrupting the ossicular chain and tympanic membrane", "Otosclerosis causing sensorineural hearing loss; bony overgrowth of the semicircular canals and oval window", "Cerumen impaction causing conductive hearing loss; the physical barrier prevents sound waves from reaching the tympanic membrane, resulting in bone conduction bypass lateralizing to the affected side", "Acoustic neuroma causing conductive hearing loss; tumor compression of the facial nerve and internal auditory canal"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient presents with a **conductive hearing loss** in the affected ear, as demonstrated by the **Weber test lateralizing to the affected ear** and a **negative Rinne test (BC > AC)** in that same ear. Conductive hearing loss is caused by a defect in the sound-conducting mechanism (external canal, tympanic membrane, or ossicles). **Cerumen impaction** is the most common cause of conductive hearing loss in older adults, affecting up to 57% of nursing home residents. Removing the cerumen (via irrigation, cerumenolytics, or manual extraction) restores hearing. Presbycusis is a sensorineural hearing loss, which would show Weber lateralizing to the *normal* ear and Rinne *positive (AC > BC)* in both ears. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female named Ruth presents with a chief complaint of sudden worsening of hearing in her right ear over the past 24 hours. Physical examination reveals:
• Weber test: **Lateralizes to the right ear**
• Rinne test: **Bone conduction > Air conduction in the right ear; Air conduction > Bone conduction in the opposite ear**
